<Hook>

A hand can have respectable showdown equity yet repeatedly fold before the river when out of position.

</Hook>

<Idea>

### Trace what prevents the showdown benchmark

Imagine holding a draw with useful raw equity. If a later bet makes continuing unprofitable, your strategy folds and gives up those potential showdown outcomes. Now imagine having position: you see the opponent check before deciding, which may let you reach another card at no additional cost. The cards' raw runouts have not changed, but the action paths have.

This is why realization is tied to a strategy and a situation. It cannot be read from a starting-hand name alone. The capture factor compresses many paths into one assumed multiplier. That can make a sensitivity exercise manageable, but it hides which folds, bets, and payments created the change.

Use the toy model by varying the factor and observing when the conclusion changes. If a small adjustment reverses the preferred action, the result depends heavily on that assumption. When you can describe the actual future choices, return to a branch-by-branch EV tree instead of treating the factor as a measured fact.

### Define the showdown benchmark

Raw equity assumes the hand reaches showdown against the modeled opponents. Equity realization compares what a strategy actually captures with that benchmark. Position affects information and control: acting last lets you observe a bet or a check before choosing your response. Out of position, a weak draw may face a price that prevents it from using its full showdown equity.

### Use a capture factor as an assumption

One illustrative model multiplies raw equity by a capture factor. <RealizationInputs /> The notebook turns the reduced average into a binary toy payoff so you can simulate it. The factor is an assumption, not something determined by hole cards alone.

### Recognize what the factor hides

Realization is not always below one: position, folds, and additional value bets can produce more profit than a passive showdown benchmark. A single scalar hides which paths changed. Prefer an explicit action tree when you know those paths; use a factor only to test sensitivity. The simplified range coach cannot infer a guaranteed capture factor from its equity sample.

